meta {
  name: Provider feedback — unsigned is rejected
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/internal/providers/beta/feedback
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  content-type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "reference_id": "does-not-exist",
    "status": "success",
    "replay": "QmxhY2tsaXN0IFN0YXR1czogQ2xlYW4="
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses an unsigned payload; never trusts an inbound webhook by default", function () {
    // No provider is configured with a webhook secret in a bare dev environment, so this route
    // returns 404 (unknown provider) rather than 200 -- either way it must never be 200 with the
    // payload accepted, which is the one outcome that would let a forged "Clean" through.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.not.equal(200);
  });

  test("gives no detail about why it was rejected", function () {
    // Telling a forger which half of the verification failed is free reconnaissance.
    const body = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(body).to.not.match(/signature|hmac|secret/i);
  });
}

docs {
  This is NOT a route a legitimate client ever calls — it is the inbound webhook a supplier's own
  infrastructure POSTs to, unauthenticated, claiming "here is the answer to order X". It is
  included here to prove the negative: an unsigned or unmatched payload must never be able to
  change a stored report.

  The real gates, in order (see `apps/api/src/routes/provider-feedback.ts`):
    1. The named provider must exist and support webhooks (`beta` does; unconfigured → 404).
    2. Its adapter must verify an HMAC signature over the RAW request bytes
       (`x-dhru-signature` header) using a secret only we and the supplier share.
    3. The `reference_id` must match an order WE placed and that is still open — otherwise the
       response is a bare 202 with no further action, so a forger cannot tell which check they
       almost hit.

  A signed, matched request is not exercisable from Bruno without a live async order and a shared
  secret, so it is not modelled here — see `packages/providers/test/webhook.test.ts` for that path
  under full control.
}
